# State-matched one-pass hashing analysis v1

## Result

Both frozen endpoints met the wall-time and retained-state gates but failed the
unchanged quality gate:

| endpoint | median macro-F1 | loss vs full | wall ratio | NumPy state | joint gate |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |
| 2^14, no averaging | 0.611684 | 0.063864 | 0.6618 | 2,621,920 B | fail: quality |
| 2^12, averaging | 0.571189 | 0.104358 | 0.5618 | 1,966,880 B | fail: quality |

The fixed full sparse reference is macro-F1 0.675548, median wall time 2.5224
seconds, and 2,847,680 coefficient/intercept bytes.  The experiment peak RSS
was 298,057,728 bytes, below both the 4 GiB process limit and 8 GB project cap.

## Interpretation

State matching repairs the oversized-state and speed defects of the 2^18
averaged endpoint, but the resulting collision/optimization regime loses more
than the predeclared 0.05 macro-F1 tolerance.  The no-averaging arm is the less
bad endpoint yet still misses the quality gate by 0.013864.  The averaged arm
at one quarter of its dimension is smaller and faster but substantially worse.

This closes one-pass hashing as an application candidate on the exposed 20
Newsgroups protocol.  It does not show that hashing, online IDF, or streaming
moments fail on larger streams, and the three order seeds do not validate a
nominal failure probability.  Further tuning against this already inspected
test split would violate the frozen close rule.

## Decision

Retire `model-application-state-matched-streaming-v1`.  Before any further
model-application experiment, audit a source-defined larger sparse/streaming
benchmark whose full sparse state or workload is genuinely consequential under
the CPU/8 GB authorization.  The audit must pin source, license/redistribution,
split, labels/metric, sparse footprint, download cost and a bounded calibration;
it may reject the benchmark without running a large numerical job.
